message.content:
{
  "message": "Fixed duplicate decision IDs by replacing the decisions array with a deduplicated version, and added missing consumer scenarios (S1, S2, S3) and an end-to-end scenario (E1) to satisfy rule 8. The contract now includes scenarios covering weight conversion, size selection, address parsing, phone normalization, idempotency, parcel vs pallet routing, webhook handling, and the full paid-order → shipment → webhook flow. No further mismatches remain from the provider side.",
  "patches": [
    {
      "op": "replace",
      "path": "/decisions",
      "value": [
        {
          "id": "D7",
          "text": "Postcode-city mismatch: my system validates that the postcode area (e.g. SW from SW1A 1AA) matches the city's known area. Mismatch returns 422 POSTCODE_CITY_MISMATCH. Should the consumer validate this client-side, or rely on provider's 422? Answer: Provider validates and returns 422; consumer handles error (provider owns the rule)"
        },
        {
          "id": "H5",
          "text": "'Shipment status to Spokes order status mapping: Parcelly emits CREATED, COLLECTED, IN_TRANSIT, DELIVERED, RETURNED. Spokes order statuses are pending, paid, shipped, delivered, cancelled. Which mapping should we lock in? Option A maps CREATED→paid (shipment created when order paid), COLLECTED/IN_TRANSIT→shipped, DELIVERED→delivered, RETURNED→cancelled. Option B maps CREATED→pending, COLLECTED→shipped, IN_TRANSIT→shipped, DELIVERED→delivered, RETURNED→cancelled. Option C lets Spokes decide per webhook. Answer: A: CREATED→paid, COLLECTED/IN_TRANSIT→shipped, DELIVERED→delivered, RETURNED→cancelled'"
        },
        {
          "id": "H6",
          "text": "Address parsing: Spokes has a single address_line (e.g. 'Long Street 5, E1 6AN, London'). Parcelly requires structured address (street, building, flat, postcode, city, country=GB). Who parses the address_line into the structured fields? Option A: Spokes parses before calling Parcelly. Option B: Parcelly accepts address_line and parses it (requires API change). Option C: Contract defines a parsing rule (e.g. split by comma, last two parts are postcode and city). Answer: A: Spokes parses address_line into street/building/flat/postcode/city before calling create_shipment"
        },
        {
          "id": "H4",
          "text": "Phone normalization: customers enter phones in many formats (07700 900200, +447700900200, 0044 7700-900200, etc.). All UK. Should we (A) normalize to +447XXXXXXXXX at input time and store normalized, (B) store as-is and normalize only when sending to Parcelly, or (C) validate format at input and reject non-conforming? Default: B Answer: B: Store as-is, normalize only when sending to Parcelly"
        },
        {
          "id": "H3",
          "text": "Address parsing at checkout: customers type whole address into one free-text field (e.g. 'Behind the old mill, London' or '10 Example Rd, London E1 6AN'). Postcode always last after city in UK format. Should we (A) parse free-text into structured fields at checkout time with customer confirmation, (B) parse automatically before sending to Parcelly and log failures for manual review, or (C) add structured address fields to checkout UI and deprecate free-text? Default: B Answer: B: Parse automatically before sending, log failures for manual review"
        },
        {
          "id": "H7",
          "text": "Weight conversion: Spokes stores weight_kg as float (e.g. 0.3, 14.5, 27.0) rounded to 3 decimals as sum of unit_weight_kg * quantity. Parcelly expects weight_g as integer grams. Conversion: weight_g = Math.round(weight_kg * 1000). Must hold for accessories (few hundred grams) and cargo bikes (>25kg).",
          "owner": "consumer"
        },
        {
          "id": "H8",
          "text": "Parcel size selection: Parcelly parcel endpoint requires size A, B, or C matching weight tier: A ≤5000g, B 5001-15000g, C 15001-25000g. Mismatch returns 422 SIZE_WEIGHT_MISMATCH. Spokes must select size based on computed weight_g before calling create_shipment.",
          "owner": "consumer"
        },
        {
          "id": "H9",
          "text": "Parcel vs pallet routing: Parcelly parcel weight must not exceed 25000g; heavier items must use pallet endpoint. Pallet weight range 25001g to 1000000g inclusive; exactly 25000g is invalid for pallet (returns 422 PALLET_WEIGHT_OUT_OF_RANGE). Spokes Haul 2 is 27kg (27000g) and 31.5kg with front rack — always pallet. Spokes must route to correct endpoint based on weight_g.",
          "owner": "consumer"
        },
        {
          "id": "H10",
          "text": "Idempotency key usage: Parcelly create_shipment/create_pallet accept Idempotency-Key header (≤255 chars, scoped per partner). Blank/missing key causes new shipment each retry → duplicate charges. Payment gateway may retry confirmations. Spokes must use order reference (e.g. ORD-1001) as Idempotency-Key when creating shipment after payment, so retries are safe.",
          "owner": "consumer"
        },
        {
          "id": "H11",
          "text": "Order status mapping from shipment webhooks: Parcelly webhook shipment.status_changed delivers status transitions. Spokes must map to order status lifecycle (pending → paid → shipped → delivered, cancelled branch from pending). Mapping: CREATED→paid (shipment created after payment), COLLECTED→shipped, IN_TRANSIT→shipped, DELIVERED→delivered, RETURNED→cancelled. Handlers must be idempotent as webhooks may retry.",
          "owner": "consumer"
        },
        {
          "id": "H7-2",
          "text": "Confirm weight conversion: weight_g = round(weight_kg * 1000) where weight_kg is already rounded to 3 decimals? Answer: Yes, round(kg*1000)",
          "owner": "consumer",
          "origin_question": "Q7"
        },
        {
          "id": "H8-2",
          "text": "Confirm parcel size selection logic: A if weight_g ≤ 5000, B if 5001-15000, C if 15001-25000? Answer: Yes",
          "owner": "consumer",
          "origin_question": "Q8"
        }
      ]
    },
