{
  "message": "I've analyzed the Parcelly provider API against our Spokes order system. Key mismatches: phone format (we store raw, they need +447XXXXXXXXX), address structure (we have free-text, they need structured fields), weight units (we use kg floats, they need integer grams with parcel/pallet split at 25kg), idempotency strategy, webhook handling, and status mapping. I've added consumer decisions for each mismatch (who converts what), consumer scenarios testing our edge cases, and 3 e2e scenarios for the main flows. Two business questions remain about incomplete addresses and multi-item weight calculation.",
  "patches": [
    {
      "op": "add",
      "path": "/decisions/-",
      "value": {
        "id": "D26",
        "text": "Consumer normalizes phone numbers to E.164 +447XXXXXXXXX format before sending to provider. Source formats include: 07700 900200, +447700900200, 0044 7700-900200, +447700900300, +447700900201, +447700900202, +447700900203, +447700900204, +447700900205. Conversion strips non-digits, ensures +447 prefix with 9 digits.",
        "owner": "consumer"
      }
    },
    {
      "op": "add",
      "path": "/decisions/-",
      "value": {
        "id": "D27",
        "text": "Consumer parses free-text address_line into provider's structured fields (street, building, postcode, city, country=GB). Postcode is last token after city in UK format (e.g., 'E1 6AN'). Building extracted as leading number from street. Incomplete addresses (e.g., 'Behind the old mill, London') cannot be fully parsed.",
        "owner": "consumer"
      }
    },
    {
      "op": "add",
      "path": "/decisions/-",
      "value": {
        "id": "D28",
        "text": "Consumer converts order total weight from kg (float, 3 decimals) to grams (integer) for provider. Parcel endpoint used when weight_g ≤ 25000; pallet endpoint when weight_g ≥ 25001. Cargo bikes >25kg (e.g., Spokes Haul 2 at 27kg, 31.5kg with rack) go to pallet.",
        "owner": "consumer"
      }
    },
